## ABSTRACT
In the pursuit of heterogeneous catalysts with high reactivity, metal organic framework (MOF) nanomaterials have received tremendous attentions. However, many MOF catalysts especially Fe-based MOFs need to be utilized immediately after synthesis or being activated using high temperature, because of the easy loss of reactivity in humid environments resulting from the occupation of active Fe sites by water molecules. Here, we describe an inspiring strategy of growing MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles inside the three-dimensional confined space of graphene aerogel (GA), generating shapeable GA/MIL-101-Fe nanocomposite convenient for practical use. Compared to MIL-101-Fe, GA/MIL-101-Fe as catalyst demonstrates much higher reactivity in Fenton-like reaction, attributing to smaller MIL-101-Fe particle size, presence of active Fe(II) sites, and abundant defects in GA. Strikingly, the weakly hydrophobic nature of the composite greatly inhibits the loss of catalytic reactivity after being stored in humid air and accelerates the recovery of reactivity in mild temperature, by resisting the entrance of water molecules and helping to exclude water molecules. This work demonstrates that a delicate design of nanocomposite structure could not only improve the reactivity of the catalytic component, but also overcome its intrinsic drawback by taking advantage of the properties of host. We hope this functional nanoconfinement strategy could be extended to more scenarios in other fields.
## KEYWORDS
nanoconfinement, hydrophobicity, MIL-101-Fe, graphene aerogel, Fenton-like reaction
## 1 Introduction
The rapid development of the global economy and industrialization produces synthetic materials with larger amount and higher complexity than ever before [1]. Many of these synthetic materials, especially organic ones, are long-term residual, bio-accumulative, toxic, and capable of long-range transport in water medium, posing great challenge to the ecological safety and human health [2]. Fenton reaction and its derived Fenton-like reactions, the most representative chemical oxidation technologies, have received a lot of attentions in both industry and academia because they could generate highly oxidative hydroxyl radicals (•OH) to completely mineralize these organic pollutants [3, 4]. The original version of Fenton reaction uses \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions and hydrogen peroxide [5], suffering several key drawbacks including narrow pH conditions \((\mathrm{pH}<4)\), slow reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions, and Fe sludge production with unrecoverable catalyst [6]. A practical solution is to design heterogeneous iron-based catalysts in the so-called Fenton-like reactions, which could greatly expand the suitable pH condition to near neutral, prevent the generation of Fe sludge, and allow easy recycle and reuse of the catalysts [7]. Recently, a lot of efforts have been made on the design of various heterogeneous iron-based catalysts with improved reactivity and enhanced stability in Fenton-like reactions [8–10].  Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) [11], constructed from metal ions and organic ligands, have recently received tremendous research attentions because of their highly controllable molecular structure and fascinating properties, including open porous structures, large surface areas, and abundant metal-bonded active sites [12]. MOFs have thus been studied for a variety of applications such as drug delivery [13], gas adsorption/separation [14], and heterogeneous catalysts [15]. For the purpose of degradation of pollutants in water, iron-based MOFs especially MIL-Fe as catalysts have shown good activity in Fenton-like reactions [16–18]. The catalytic reactivity of iron-based MOFs results from the unsaturated Lewis acid Fe sites, which could adsorb oxidant, such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), to form \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-Fe complex [19]. The electron transfer between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and Fe sites promotes the reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) as well as the formation of •OH [20]. However, most Fe sites in MIL-Fe exist as \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\), which has much lower reactivity than \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) to activate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [21]. Meanwhile, under ambient conditions, these coordinatively unsaturated Fe sites could be easily occupied by water molecules [22], inhibiting the reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\). Moreover,
