Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) [11], constructed from metal ions and organic ligands, have recently received tremendous research attentions because of their highly controllable molecular structure and fascinating properties, including open porous structures, large surface areas, and abundant metal-bonded active sites [12]. MOFs have thus been studied for a variety of applications such as drug delivery [13], gas adsorption/separation [14], and heterogeneous catalysts [15]. For the purpose of degradation of pollutants in water, iron-based MOFs especially MIL-Fe as catalysts have shown good activity in Fenton-like reactions [16–18]. The catalytic reactivity of iron-based MOFs results from the unsaturated Lewis acid Fe sites, which could adsorb oxidant, such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , to form \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) -Fe complex [19]. The electron transfer between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and Fe sites promotes the reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) as well as the formation of •OH [20]. However, most Fe sites in MIL-Fe exist as \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) , which has much lower reactivity than \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) to activate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [21]. Meanwhile, under ambient conditions, these coordinatively unsaturated Fe sites could be easily occupied by water molecules [22], inhibiting the reduction of \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) . Moreover,
the long-term use of MIL-Fe in Fenton-like reaction is also challenged by a high tendency to aggregate, which prohibits the transport of reactive oxygen species [23]. In order to address some of these issues, Tang and Wang [16] reported a vacuum drying treatment at high temperature \((230\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) on MIL-100-Fe catalyst, in order to remove the coordinated water molecules and to create coordinatively unsaturated \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) sites, exhibiting high reactivity in Fenton-like reaction for degradation of sulfamethazine. Recently, Ahmad et al. [18] reported a vacuum drying treatment at \(205\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) on nanocomposite of MIL-88B-Fe and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\), to eliminate the coordinated water molecules and create mixed \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})/\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) sites, demonstrating excellent reactivity in the photo-Fenton degradation of sulfamethoxazole. Consequently, a design of nanocomposites containing iron-based MOFs with intrinsic \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})/\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) sites as well as high resistance to ambient water molecules is of fundamental importance to solve the above issues, and anticipated to push its real application a step forward.
Graphene aerogel (GA) is carbon aerogel with three-dimensional (3D) porous network structure derived from graphene nanomaterials [24, 25]. Compared with traditional graphene materials, GA possesses large specific surface area, rich pore structure, chemical stability, and environmental compatibility, demonstrating its great potential to be substrate of metal catalyst [26]. Moreover, the use of GA as confined host for active catalytic component could improve the catalytic reactivity via electron transfer and reduce the poisoning of catalyst by excluding the water molecules in humid atmosphere, because of the \(\pi{-}\pi\) conjugated structure and surface hydrophobicity of GA [27]. Here, we would like to use the term "nanoconfinement" to reflect that the active species are restricted in the confined space provided by the substrate, and to emphasize that this confinement is on the scale of nanometers. Although the fundamentals need further investigations, the nanoconfinement has recently been considered as an effective and promising strategy to advance various water treatment technologies [28]. We thus anticipate that the use of GA as nanoconfinement host for iron-based MOFs could give rise to substantial change to their structure, properties, and reactivity in Fenton-like reaction.
In this study, we describe a nanoconfinement of MIL-101-Fe inside GA, generating shapeable GA/MIL-101-Fe composite. In the subsequent Fenton-like reaction for the degradation of a model pollutant phenol, the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite as catalyst exhibited much improved catalytic reactivity than the bare MIL-101-Fe. Strikingly, compared to MIL-101-Fe, the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite also exhibited much enhanced stability in humid air as well as easier recovery of reactivity under mild condition, because of the weakly hydrophobic nature of the GA host. Through the current proof-of-concept study, we would like to emphasize that for water treatment application, the structure, property, and microenvironment of the host materials are of paramount importance in the design of nanocomposite for the nanoconfinement of active nanomaterials.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and characterizations
Graphene oxide (GO) powder sample was purchased from Suzhou Tan Feng Graphene Technology Co., Ltd., China. 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC), ferric chloride \(\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\) , ethane diamine (EDA), \(N,N\) -dimethyl formamide (DMF), phenol, bisphenol A (BPA), tetracycline (TC), benzoic acid (BA), hydrogen peroxide \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , \(30\%\) ),
methyl alcohol (MeOH), sodium chloride (NaCl), sodium sulfate \(\mathrm{(Na_{2}S O_{4})}\) , sodium nitrate \(\mathrm{(NaNO_{3})}\) , humic acid (HA), hydrochloric acid (HCl), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), and 5,5-dimethyl-1- pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) were all purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation, China. All commercial reagents were used as received.
## 2.2 Synthesis of GA
GA sample was prepared via a widely adopted one-step hydrothermal method reported previously [29]. In a typical synthesis, \(30~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{GO}\) powder and \(0.03\mathrm{~mL~}\) EDA were added into \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water (DI). Afterward, the mixture was treated by ultrasonication for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and then transferred into a \(100~\mathrm{{mL}}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and maintained at \(180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . After cooling to \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) a hydrogel sample was obtained and immersed in \(15\%\) ethanol solution for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) to remove residual precursor material. Finally, the sample was treated by freeze drying for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to form GA sample.
## 2.3 Synthesis of GA/MIL-101-Fe and MIL-101-Fe
The obtained GA sample (ca. \(30~\mathrm{mg}\) ) was placed in a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF solution containing \(540\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{FeC}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(996\;\mathrm{mg}\) BDC under constant stirring for \(30\;\mathrm{\min}\) . The mixture was then transferred to a \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The product was washed by DMF, ethanol, and water three times, followed by freeze drying for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) to generate GA/MIL-101-Fe. For comparison, an MIL-101-Fe orange powder sample was prepared via the similar method without the addition of GA.
## 2.4 Catalytic reactions
The Fenton-like reactions using GA/MIL-101-Fe as catalyst were carried out in a serious of \(100\;\mathrm{mL}\) beaker, with \(\mathrm{pH}\) values adjusted by \(0.1\,\mathrm{\mM\NaOH}\) or HCl. In a typical reaction, \(5\,\mathrm{mg}\) GA/MIL-101-Fe was added in \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) solution containing \(0.1\,\mathrm{\mM}\) target molecules phenol, with \(\mathrm{pH}\) value adjusted to 5.0 followed by addition of \(6\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to initiate the reaction, under constant stirring. Afterward, \(1.0~\mathrm{mL}\) aliquot of solution was removed at different time, and filtered through a \(0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) Millipore filter, quenched immediately by adding \(0.03\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) MeOH for further determination of the concentration of residue phenol. In the end of the degradation, the total organic carbon (TOC) of the solution was measured. In our study, effects of several experimental parameters were investigated, including the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\) , dosage of catalyst and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , and the presence of other anions. We evaluated the stability of reactivity of MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe in humid environment by using the catalysts after being stored in \(85\%\) relative humidity for different time. For quenching experiments of probing the reactive species, excessive MeOH was added into the reaction. In addition, the degradation of different organic pollutants including BPA, TC, and BA, using GA/MIL-101-Fe as catalyst was also evaluated.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of materials
Figure 1(a) is the schematic illustration of the design of the GA/MIL-101-Fe sample, showing the use of GA as substrate for the growth of MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles. The optical images of a reference MIL-101-Fe sample, a GA sample, and a GA/MIL-101-Fe composite sample are shown in Figs. 1(b)–1(d).
One sees that the orange MIL-101-Fe sample is essentially powder-like without processable shape. The use of shapeable GA with millimeter size allows the inside accommodation of MIL-101-Fe, resulting in GA/MIL-101-Fe composite retaining the original shape of GA. From the representative scanning electron microscope (SEM) images, we could see pure octahedrons with sizes ranging from \(500\,\mathrm{nm}\) to \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) of MIL-101-Fe (Fig. 1(e)), and the distribution of apparently smaller octahedrons with sizes ranging from 200 to \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) on the surface of GA sheets of GA/MIL-101-Fe (Fig. 1(f)). A representative transmission electron microscope (TEM) image of GA/MIL-101-Fe shows clearly an MIL-101-Fe cube surrounded by the GA matrix (Fig. 1(g)). These results suggest that for the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite, GA as substrate not only provides macroscopic shape for the accommodation of MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles, but also creates microscopic nanoconfinement for the growth of MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles, resulting in smaller MIL-101-Fe particles than those prepared without a substrate. Smaller MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles should be favored for higher catalytic reactivity.
We then characterized the crystalline structure, chemical structure, and compositions of GA, MIL-101-Fe, and GA/ MIL-101-Fe samples. Figure 2(a) shows two characteristic diffraction peaks at \(8.9^{\circ}\), \(9.2^{\circ}\), and \(10.7^{\circ}\) of MIL-101-Fe [30] in both MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe samples. Meanwhile, GA/MIL-101-Fe also exhibits a broad peak of amorphous reduced graphene oxide sheets centered at \(25.3^{\circ}\), which is the major characteristic peak of GA. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra in Fig. 2(b) show that for both MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe containing BDC as organic linker, the adsorption peaks at 1,572 and \(1,505\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are attributed to the asymmetric stretching of \(\mathrm{O-H}\), the peak at \(1,381\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to the symmetric stretching of \(\mathrm{O-H}\), and the peak at \(746\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is attributed to the out-of-plane bending vibration of \(\mathrm{C-H}\) in the benzene ring of BDC [31]. One might also notice that the peak intensities of GA/MIL-101-Fe are apparently lower than those of MIL-101-Fe. Moreover, the GA sample shows similar spectra as reported previously [32]. Figure 2(c) shows the thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA) curves of the three samples in air atmosphere. GA experienced a complete loss \((100\%)\) of mass at \(570\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), while MIL-101-Fe lost \(60.8\%\) at \(440\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with remaining \(39.2\%\). Based on the final weight loss of \(89.2\%\) of GA/MIL-101-Fe (remaining \(10.8\%\)), we thus could calculate the fraction of MIL-101-Fe to be \(10.8/39.2=27.6\%\) in the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite. All these results suggest the
accommodation of MIL-101-Fe inside the GA host.
We also examined the surface hydrophilicity of the final GA/MIL-101-Fe composite in comparison to the MIL-101-Fe powders. The optical images in Fig. 2(d) show the water contact angles at different contact time. We can see that the water contact angle of MIL-101-Fe is only \(61^{\circ}\), which quickly drops to ca. \(20^{\circ}\) s later, suggesting a strong hydrophilicity. In contrast, the water contact angle of GA/MIL-101-Fe remains almost unchanged at ca. \(100^{\circ}\) in \(5\,\mathrm{s}\), implying a weak hydrophobicity of its surface. We anticipate that the weakly hydrophobic surface should effectively inhibit the entrance of water molecules in humid air, however, we do not want the surface hydrophobicity of GA/MIL-101-Fe to be too strong, which might inhibit the mass transfer of pollutants for subsequent reaction in water.
## 3.2 Catalytic reactivity in Fenton-like reactions
We proceeded to evaluate the catalytic reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe with other comparative samples in Fenton-like reactions for the degradation of a model pollutant phenol. Figure 3(a) shows the evolution of phenol concentration \((C/C_{0}\), where \(C_{0}\) is the initial concentration) vs. time in different systems at \(\mathrm{pH}=5.0\), which is impossible for homogenous Fenton reactions [33]. An additional set of adsorption experiments show that both MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe samples exhibit negligible adsorption of phenol \((<6\%\), see Fig. S1 in the ESM). One sees that both \(\mathrm{GA/H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{GA/Fe^{3+}/H_{2}O_{2}}\) systems are not effective for the phenol degradation (only \(<7\%\) removal in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), presumably due to the adsorption). The \(\mathrm{MIL-101-Fe/H_{2}O_{2}}\) system is able to eliminate phenol by \(46\%\) in \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) and \(99\%\) in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), while the \(\mathrm{GA/MIL-101-Fe/H_{2}O_{2}}\) system could remove \(97\%\) in \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) and \(99\%\) in \(40\,\mathrm{min}\). The removal of TOCs in both systems is shown in Fig. S2 in the ESM, which also demonstrates a higher efficient TOC removal by GA/MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) than MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\). The degradation data are replotted in Fig. 3(b) and fitted to pseudo-first-order kinetics model (see details in the ESM), from which we could see that the GA/MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) exhibits a rate constant of \(0.158\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), which is 3.7 times of that \((0.043\,\mathrm{min}^{-1})\) in MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\). It is important to notice that GA alone exhibits negligible reactivity, thus, the active component of GA/MIL-101-Fe is solely MIL-101-Fe. Remarkably, with the same catalyst dosage of \(5.0\,\mathrm{mg}\), GA/MIL-101-Fe with only \(27.6\%\) mass fraction of active MIL-101-Fe component demonstrates much higher
reactivity than pure MIL-101-Fe. Moreover, we would like to emphasize that both GA/MIL-101-Fe and MIL-101-Fe samples exhibit excellent catalytic reactivity, given the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of 5.0, under which homogeneous Fenton reaction is not applicable. These results suggest that the use of GA as the host for MIL-101-Fe could greatly improve the catalytic reactivity in Fenton-like reaction. The systematic investigations of the effects of various experimental parameters, i.e., solution \(\mathrm{pH}\), dosage of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), presence of coexisting anions and organic substances, on the catalytic reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe in Fenton-like reactions are presented in Figs. S3–S5 in the ESM, with accompanying discussion.
We are intrigued by the dramatic improvement of the catalytic reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe composite in comparison to bare MIL-101-Fe. One should acquire crucial information about their activity by examining the chemical status of Fe element, which is the key catalytic species involved in Fenton-like reactions [34]. We investigated the chemical status of Fe element by both Mössbauer and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), as shown in Figs. 3(c)–3(e). From the Mössbauer spectra of MIL-101-Fe (Fig. 3(c)) and GA/MIL-101-Fe (Fig. 3(d), one could see central doublets in both samples. In comparison to MIL-101-Fe, GA/MIL-101-Fe exhibits a new doublet with an isomer shift (IS) of ca. \(0.45\;\mathrm{mm\cdot s^{-1}}\) and a quadrupole splitting (QS) of ca. \(1.79\;\mathrm{mm\cdot s^{-1}}\), which are the characteristic signals of a low-spin Fe(II) species. Meanwhile, the spectra confirm the presence of Fe(III) species showing the uniform IS of ca. \(0.40\;\mathrm{mm\cdot s^{-1}}\), combined with the QS of the fitted doublets in the range of \(0.34{-}1.06\;\mathrm{mm\cdot s^{-1}}\) in both MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe [35, 36]. Moreover, The deconvolutions of XPS Fe 2p spectra of MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe are shown in Fig. 3(e). One sees that MIL-101-Fe exhibits characteristic Fe(III) peaks at 727.6, 724.7, 718.3, 713.6, and \(711.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) [16]. Interestingly, for GA/MIL-101-Fe, one could not only see all these Fe(III) peaks with slight shifts, but also observe a new peak at \(710.7\;\mathrm{eV}\), which is attributed to the binding energy of Fe(II) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) [37]. It is a well-accepted sense that Fe(II) has a much higher reactivity than Fe(III) for the activation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [38, 39]. These results suggest that the synthesis of MIL-101-Fe inside the GA host creates Fe(II) sites that are hardly detected in bare MIL-101-Fe samples, resulting in a much improved reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe composite than bare MIL-101-Fe.
We highly suspect that the GA host also makes substantial contribution to the improvement of the catalytic reactivity of MIL-101-Fe. In order to verify our hypothesis, we carried out a Raman characterization on GA and GA/MIL-101-Fe samples, and their spectra are shown in Fig. 3(f). Both samples exhibit peaks at 1,337 and \(1,581\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), corresponding to the characteristic D and G bands of typical carbon materials [40]. It is more informative to evaluate the ratio of the intensity of D and G peaks, \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\), which reflects the density of defects in carbon materials. The higher the ratio is, the more defect sites the sample possesses. Abundant defects in carbon materials normally result in higher reactivity in Fenton-like reactions, because of the enhanced electron transfer between the carbon substrate and the catalytic reactive center [41]. One could see that GA/MIL-101-Fe exhibits a much higher \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) ratio of 1.20, than that (1.03) of pristine GA. The abundant defects of GA in GA/MIL-101-Fe should contribute significantly to its excellent reactivity.
## 3.3 Stability of catalysts
We are especially interested in the reactivity of the MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe catalysts after storage in normal humid environment. We intentionally kept both samples in open air with constant \(85\%\) relative humidity, and then examined their activity at different time. The data of phenol degradation by using MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe catalysts from 1 to 7 days are presented in Figs. 4(a) and 4(b). One sees that MIL-101-Fe experiences a dramatic loss of its reactivity as the time in humid air increases. At the \(7^{\mathrm{th}}\) day, MIL-101-Fe could only remove ca. \(20\%\) phenol in \(60\;\mathrm{min}\). However, as the elapse of time in humid air, the reduction of the reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe is much slower. At the \(7^{\mathrm{th}}\) day, GA/MIL-101-Fe is still able to remove \(99\%\) phenol in \(60\;\mathrm{min}\), but with slower rate. The values of rate constants of these reactions are plotted in Fig. 4(c). One could see that both samples experience a decrease of the catalytic reactivity when placed in humid air. However, the loss of reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe is much slower than that of MIL-101-Fe. Meanwhile, at different storage time, GA/MIL-101-Fe exhibits much higher reactivity than MIL-101-Fe. These results strongly suggest that the use of GA as host for the accommodation of MIL-101-Fe could greatly improve the stability of the catalysts. Given the results of water contact angles in Fig. 2(d), we highly suspect that compared
to the hydrophilic MIL-101-Fe powder that easily adsorbs water molecules and loses reactivity, the weakly hydrophobic nature of the GA host could effectively inhibit the adsorption of water molecules and mitigate the loss of reactivity of GA/MIL-101-Fe.
We further carried out a set of experiments by evaluating the reactivity of MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe at 180 days after being placed in humid environment. Results in Fig. 4(d) show that both samples lost their reactivity, exhibiting only \(11\%\) (MIL-101-Fe) and \(17\%\) (GA/MIL-101-Fe) phenol removal in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\). We then simply placed the two samples in a vacuum oven at a mild temperature of only \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and re-examined their reactivity. We could see from Fig. 4(d) that both samples recovered a fraction of their original reactivity. The MIL-101-Fe was able to degrade \(31\%\) phenol in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), while strikingly, the GA/MIL-101-Fe could degrade \(82\%\) phenol in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\). Based on these results, we could conclude that the weakly hydrophobic GA host could also help to repel the adsorbed water molecules, promoting the recovery of the reactivity of the catalyst.
We characterized both MIL-101-Fe and GA/MIL-101-Fe samples after being placed in humid environment \((85\%\) RH) for 180 days by both X-ray diffraction (XRD) and XPS. Results in Fig. S6 in the ESM show that one cannot observe obvious differences with the newly-synthesized GA/MIL-101-Fe. These results might imply that the adsorption of water molecules does not change the intrinsic chemistry of these materials, explaining the recovery of reactivity after desorption. We further evaluated the stability of GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst by measuring the leached Fe ions at different \(\mathrm{pH}\) conditions, as well as the reusability of the GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst. Figure S7 in the ESM shows that the concentrations of leached Fe from GA/MIL-101-Fe are ca. \(0.16\,\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}=4.0–7.0\), corresponding to a fraction of \(2.2\%\) of the original Fe in the catalyst. Data in Fig. S8 in the ESM show the use of GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst in four sequential Fenton-like reactions, demonstrating \(99\%\), \(88\%\), \(87\%\), and \(86\%\) phenol removal in \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), respectively. After the catalytic reaction, the GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst was re-examined by both SEM and XRD. Results in Fig. S9 in the ESM show that no significant change to
the morphology and crystalline structure is observed after the use. All these results suggest a good stability of GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst in reaction.
## 3.4 Detection of reactive oxidative species (ROS)
We then carried out experiments to detect the ROS generated in the GA/MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) system that accounted for the degradation of phenol. Data in Fig. 5(a) show that, after the addition of excessive •OH radical scavenger methanol into the system, the degradation of phenol is completely suppressed. The final removal rate at \(60\,\mathrm{min}\) is only ca. \(6\%\), which should be attributed to the adsorption of phenol. We further investigated the ROS by adding DMPO as probing molecules in various systems. The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra in Fig. 5(b) show four peaks with intensity ratios of 1:2:2:1 in both MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) and GA/MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) systems, attributing to the characteristic signals of DMPO–•OH complex [42]. The GA/MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\) system exhibits much stronger signal than MIL-101-Fe/H\(_2\)O\(_2\), meanwhile, the signals are well suppressed by the addition of excessive methanol. All these results confirm that the ROS generated is •OH radical that degrades the phenol.
## 3.5 Further discussion on the nanoconfinement design strategy of the catalysts
MOF nanomaterials have recently attracted tremendous research attentions from scientists of various fields, including environmental scientists for the removal of pollutants. The majority of previous attempts have been focused on the examination of the remarkable properties and performances of the bare MOF nanomaterials, which are immediately utilized after synthesis. The unstable reactivity under humid environment due to the occupation of active sites by water molecules is widely acknowledged, however, deliberately ignored by many studies, which is especially important for environmental remediation application. Consequently, we reckon that novel strategies aimed to improve the ambient stability of reactivity of MOF-based nanomaterials are of great fundamental importance for environmental application.
On the other hand, the direct use of nanomaterials in real application, especially on a large scale, is challenged by many inherent issues including aggregation, difficult operation (e.g., separation and regeneration), potential risks exerted on the contact ecosystem, and etc. In order to address these issues, a practical solution is to confine these nanomaterials into host materials and framework with large processable shape. Consequently, the choice of the host materials is of extreme importance because the host would interact with these nanomaterials and modify their properties, and meanwhile, the microenvironment and the spatial nanoconfinement of the
host could greatly affect the reactions that occur inside. These so-called nanoconfinement effects are very profound in the processes that are involved in water treatment [43], and have been elaborated and speculated in a recent critical review [28].
In this work, we describe a proof-of-concept nanoconfinement strategy, as shown in Fig. 6(a), for MIL-101-Fe nanomaterials by using GA as 3D host, which transforms MIL-101-Fe powder into shapeable GA/MIL-101-Fe nanocomposite. Compared to the MIL-101-Fe nanomaterials, the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite with only \(27.6\%\) active MIL-101-Fe component exhibited much higher catalytic reactivity in Fenton-like reaction. Such improvement of reactivity was attributed to three major factors, i.e., smaller MIL-101-Fe nanoparticles, the presence of Fe(II) species in GA/MIL-101-Fe, and the abundant carbon defects of the GA host. The GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst also exhibited excellent reactivity for the degradation of various organic pollutants including BPA, TC, and BA, as shown in Fig. 6(b). More strikingly, compared to MIL-101-Fe nanomaterials, the GA/MIL-101-Fe composite demonstrated much higher stability of reactivity under humid environment as well as an easier recovery of reactivity after a mild treatment. The key reason is that rather than the hydrophilic MIL-101-Fe nanomaterials that easily absorb water molecules and lose reactivity, the weakly hydrophobic nature of GA/MIL-101-Fe efficiently inhibits the adsorption of water molecules and helps to repel water molecules during the recovery process.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, our work shows that in the design of catalysts with composite structure, a rational selection of the host for the nanoconfinement of nanomaterials could not only greatly increase the reactivity of nanomaterials, but also improve the ambient stability of reactivity of the catalysts by taking advantage of the unique surface property of the host. The excellent reactivity of the GA/MIL-101-Fe catalyst is highlighted by a comprehensive comparison of the shorter time needed for pollutant removal to those in previous studies under similar conditions (see Table S1 in the ESM for details). A key message from this study is that the use of nanoconfinement results in comprehensive changes from various aspects to the catalysts. The purpose of our study is not to provide an ultimate solution for the real application of these materials for industrial use, but rather, to use MIL-101-Fe as a case to provide a novel perspective for the design of nanocomposite with similar issues that better suits the scenario in real application. Future studies could be focused on the further improvement of the catalytic performance as well as the extension of use in near neutral pH conditions in real applications.
